Analyze this data and submit a structured recommendation using the submit_training_recommendation tool. Base all quantitative judgments strictly on the numbers provided above — do not invent or assume any data not present here. Use the user's age, sex, and BMI, if provided in userContext, to calibrate what counts as reasonable training volume and intensity for their profile. Use strengthIndex as your primary evidence for progressive overload. Use volumeSplit and routineAdherence together to judge balance and consistency. Factor in the user's long-term goal and this period's note (if provided) when shaping your advice and action items. If totalSets is 0, note that no training was logged this period rather than speculating why.

Respond in English. Every text field you submit must be plain prose only — no XML tags, no markdown formatting, no stray closing tags of any kind. The headline must stand completely on its own — write it as if it's the only sentence the user will ever read.

{
  "headline": "You missed half your sessions this week, so prioritizing consistency is the most important thing right now.",
  "summary": "This was a mixed week. You completed Upper A and Lower A but missed Upper B and Lower B, meaning you only hit half your planned sessions. With just 16 total sets logged, your volume was on the lower end for muscle building at your profile — a 28-year-old male at a healthy BMI of 23.5 has solid capacity for more work. The good news is the tonnage you did accumulate was meaningful, and your effort per session appears solid. However, the missed sessions directly limited your stimulus for growth this week, so consistency is the number one priority heading into the next period.",
  "progressiveOverload": {
    "status": "stalling",
    "notes": "The strengthIndex data tells a clear story this week: no muscle group made forward progress. Chest held flat at 104, back at 103, shoulders at 102, biceps at 101, and triceps at 103 — all unchanged from the previous period. Legs were the only group that moved, and they actually slipped slightly from 106 to 105, a minor regression. With half the planned sessions missed, it is very likely that the reduced frequency and volume are the main culprits behind this stall. Progressive overload cannot reliably occur when training stimulus is cut in half mid-cycle."
  },
  "muscleImbalances": [
    {
      "muscleGroup": "Shoulders",
      "severity": "mild",
      "observation": "Shoulders account for only 9.4% of volume split with just 2 sets, sitting noticeably below chest and back. For balanced upper-body development and long-term shoulder health, gradually increasing shoulder volume would be beneficial."
    },
    {
      "muscleGroup": "Biceps",
      "severity": "moderate",
      "observation": "Biceps have the lowest volume share at 4.8% and only 2 sets logged. For a muscle-building goal, this is likely insufficient direct stimulus, especially when chest and back are receiving substantially more attention. Bringing biceps volume closer to triceps levels would improve arm balance."
    }
  ],
  "deloadRecommended": false,
  "deloadReason": null,
  "actionItems": [
    "Complete all four planned sessions next period — Upper A, Upper B, Lower A, and Lower B. Consistency is the single biggest lever you can pull right now to restart progressive overload.",
    "Focus on adding a small amount of weight or an extra rep on at least one exercise per session to get the strength indexes moving upward again, starting with legs where a slight regression was seen.",
    "Add at least one additional set for biceps across the week, bringing total bicep sets to 3 or 4, to close the gap with other muscle groups and support balanced arm development.",
    "Keep recovery habits strong between sessions — sleep, nutrition, and protein intake are especially important when you are trying to build muscle and need to make the most of every session you complete."
  ],
  "contextSummary": "Only two of four planned sessions were completed this period, causing all strength indexes to stall or slightly regress; the top priority next period is full session adherence to restore progressive overload momentum."
}
